Christian,

	I don't know if this will help, since I am not sure
	what you are complaining about..

> isp0: driver initiated bus reset of bus 0
	Seems initially to be something that isn't right...
	
	First, a larger amount of physical memory normally stresses
	a FS less. What is your memory size?

	Second, pure writes tend to be asynch due to the ability of
	not waiting for the results of the writes. But most writes
	are actually read-modify-writes.. that modify an existing
	file system block.

	Third, any disk based access is a "loss" since the order
	of magnitude of acess speed is maybe 100x slower to disk
	than memory. Larger memory allows fewer required writes (due
	to lower % amount of non dirty memory) and better
	elevator ordering of writes when writes are done..

	Fourth, I am unaware of specifly the capabilities of your
	hardware combination and whether your MB/sec speeds are
	accurate.. More queued up writes should give a better MB/sec
	number while possibly increasing the latency of each of the
	writes..

	Fifth, reads are dependent on a number of factors as whether
	1000s of files that are read are located within the same
	directory, whether the created files were lengthened over
	time, whether the files have holes, whether the size of the
	files are such that in a FFS double or triple indirects
	are used, etc...

	Sixth, whether your FS access is primarily of data or text..


	Mitchell Erblich
	Sr Software Engineer
	----------------------------

	

